---
// Site-wide "Quick Access" rail: a compact vertical dock on the left edge so a
// visitor anywhere on the site can jump straight to any SITE_ENTRIES
// destination without detouring through the nav.
//
// SELF-EXPLANATION (railHeader below): a first-time visitor has no reason to
// know what this floating panel is, so it carries a badge icon and a static
// "Quick Access" title rather than opening cold on a bare icon switch, which
// read as an unstyled stray box rather than a deliberate feature. It's
// always-visible markup, not a dismissible onboarding tooltip (so it can't be
// missed once and never seen again). It used to also carry a one-line
// mode-aware description; that was dropped as bloat — the three mode segments
// directly below it already say what each dataset is.
//
// A 3-way MODE switch (the first top-toolbar control — a real segmented
// switch, all 3 options always visible at once, not a single icon-swap
// button) picks which dataset the rest of the rail shows. The three modes
// mirror the site's own navigation-by-OBJECT-TYPE split, not by domain —
// Reference (Lookup Tables / Guides & Concepts / Frameworks & Maps), Tools
// (Artifact Parsers / Decoders & Extractors / Converters & Calculators /
// Rule & Query Builders), or Drills (the one `drills` category) — never
// more than one at once. All three datasets' category blocks are rendered
// server-side (so a no-JS visitor still gets real, fully labeled links for
// whichever mode the current page belongs to — see `defaultMode` below); JS
// only ever toggles which blocks are hidden, filtered by mode alone (see
// `applyGroupVisibility()`). Desktop/tablet only (`md` and up) — on narrow
// viewports the nav's own mobile menu (flat Reference/Tools/Drills links
// straight to each section's landing page) already covers this, and a
// fixed-left rail would just overlap body copy.
//
// Two view states: hidden (panel slid fully off-screen, only the edge handle
// remains) and shown (the default — every category of the active mode,
// always labeled, always all at once). There is deliberately no third
// "icons-only" or "single category" state and no separate "show all"
// toggle — every category of whichever mode is active is ALWAYS visible,
// every link is ALWAYS labeled. This used to be optional (an "icons" default
// plus a manually-toggled "show all"/"expand" pair), gated behind a JS
// height check that disabled the all-categories view outright on a short
// window ("unavailable — window too short") since an unbounded stack of 20
// links centered via `top: 50%` on a short viewport had no scroll affordance
// to reach the overflow. That measure-and-disable approach is gone; see the
// scroll-region comment below for what replaced it and why it needed no
// disable path at all.
//
// The hide/show handle toggles hidden ⟷ shown — a small edge tab while
// hidden, docks flush at whichever edge is currently visible (left: 0/240px)
// and grows to the rail's full height once shown — all driven, animated, by
// pure CSS sibling selectors reading `rail`'s own data-view, no JS position
// math — see `handle`'s comment below.
//
// Because every category of a mode (Tools has 4, 25 links total) is always
// on screen at once, the rail's own toolbar row (drag handle + mode switch)
// stays fixed while everything below it — the actual category/link list —
// scrolls in its own internal region, capped to whatever vertical room is
// actually available below the sticky header on THIS viewport (see
// `scrollRegion`'s own comment for the exact mechanism: a CSS custom
// property the script keeps in sync, with a generous static CSS fallback for
// the brief pre-JS window and genuine no-JS visitors). This is what "an
// effective mechanism to work on all types of clients" means here: a plain
// `max-height` + `overflow-y: auto` adapts to literally any viewport height
// — a half-height split window, a short laptop screen, a tall external
// monitor, a foldable — with no JS-measured "does it fit" branch to get
// wrong, and unlike the old disable-the-feature fallback, content that
// doesn't fit scrolls into view instead of simply vanishing behind a grayed-
// out button.
//
// Per-category color coding: a distinct hue-rotation degree per group index,
// evenly spaced around the color wheel — computed separately per dataset
// since Reference's 3 categories need a different spacing than Tools'
// 4. Applied via CSS `filter: hue-rotate()` (inline `style`, since the exact
// degree is a per-item computed value Panda's static token system can't
// generate a class for) to small, isolated, childless decorative elements
// only — `catDot`/`catAccentBar` below, never `catBlock` itself, since
// `filter` recolors an element's entire rendered subtree and would also
// shift every icon/label color inside it. Rotating a single theme token
// (`primary`) this way — rather than hardcoding fixed colors — is what keeps
// this automatically distinct-but-harmonious across all 10 of the site's
// palettes: each rotation shifts relative to whatever that theme's actual
// hue already is, instead of clashing with half of them. Every category is
// always on screen at once now, so this coloring is purely static, computed
// once server-side per category — no JS ever recolors anything at runtime
// (there's no more "currently active category" concept to track).
//
// State (hidden + mode + vertical drag position) persists via localStorage,
// same "session resuming" pattern as the original tool-only rail this
// replaces.
//
// Every toggle button here is debounced (see `debounceClick` in the script
// below) — CLAUDE.md invariant 12 makes this a sitewide rule for any button
// that drives a CSS/JS transition, not just a fix local to this file.
import { css } from 'styled-system/css';
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
import { SITE_ENTRIES, groupsFor } from '../consts';

const currentPath = Astro.url.pathname;

// `groupsFor` (consts.ts) is the exact same shape Navigation.astro and the
// section index pages derive their menus from, so the rail can never drift
// out of sync with the nav. This file only ever reads a group's `category`
// and `links`; `id`/`blurb`/`intro` come along harmlessly and are consumed by
// the landing pages instead.

// Smart default: open on whichever MODE owns the current page, so a
// first-time visitor lands on a relevant list. `tools` is the fallback when
// the current page isn't under any SITE_ENTRIES destination at all (a blog
// post, the homepage) — Tools is both the largest dataset and the likeliest
// reason a visitor reaches for this rail.
//
// Matched by LONGEST href prefix, not equality: most of this site's pages are
// generated detail pages (/reference/glossary/<slug>/, /reference/attack-map/<id>/,
// /reference/event-ids/<slug>/, …) that are Reference content but never appear in
// SITE_ENTRIES themselves, so an exact match would fall through to `tools` on
// the overwhelming majority of the site. Longest-match specifically (not any
// prefix match): /tools/hash-calculator/ is under BOTH the "Tool Catalog"
// reference entry at /reference/tool-catalog/ and its own tool entry, and only the longest
// one picks the right mode. Same shape as Navigation's hrefOwner tie-break —
// an exact/deeper entry always outranks a shallower hub prefix.
const entry = SITE_ENTRIES.filter((e) => currentPath.startsWith(e.href)).sort(
  (a, b) => b.href.length - a.href.length
)[0];
const defaultMode = entry
  ? entry.type === 'reference'
    ? 'reference'
    : entry.type === 'drill'
      ? 'drills'
      : 'tools'
  : 'tools';

const GROUPS_BY_MODE = {
  reference: groupsFor('reference'),
  tools: groupsFor('tool'),
  drills: groupsFor('drill'),
};
// Evenly-spaced hue rotations, one per category index, computed per mode
// since each mode has a different category count (Reference 3, Tools 4,
// Drills 1). A single-entry hue array is just [0] (no rotation) — there's
// nothing to color-distinguish with only one category.
const HUES_BY_MODE = {
  reference: GROUPS_BY_MODE.reference.map((_, i) => Math.round((360 / GROUPS_BY_MODE.reference.length) * i)),
  tools: GROUPS_BY_MODE.tools.map((_, i) => Math.round((360 / GROUPS_BY_MODE.tools.length) * i)),
  drills: GROUPS_BY_MODE.drills.map((_, i) => Math.round((360 / GROUPS_BY_MODE.drills.length) * i)),
};

// Static — just drives the 3 segment buttons' own icon/label markup below;
// the script keys off each button's own `data-mode-value`, not this array.
const MODE_OPTIONS = [
  { value: 'reference', label: 'Reference', icon: 'book-open' },
  { value: 'tools', label: 'Tools', icon: 'wrench' },
  { value: 'drills', label: 'Drills', icon: 'target' },
] as const;
// Server-side mirror of the bundled client script's own `MODE_LABELS` —
// used to compute the hide/show handle's default-mode-aware SSR label so a
// no-JS visitor on a Drills page never sees pre-hydration HTML written for
// the wrong mode (e.g. "Hide Tools sidebar" on a page actually showing Drills).
// The client copy is a module-level const in that script rather than a
// `define:vars` injection: `define:vars` forces `is:inline`, which is what
// used to ship this whole 21KB script verbatim into all 1,730 pages instead
// of one minified, content-hashed, cached chunk.
const MODE_LABELS = { reference: 'Reference', tools: 'Tools', drills: 'Drills' } as const;


const outer = css({
  position: 'fixed',
  left: 0,
  top: '50%',
  zIndex: 45,
  display: { base: 'none', md: 'block' },
});

// Shared between `rail`'s own width and `handle`'s `left` docking offset
// below, so the two can never drift out of sync the way a pair of
// hand-duplicated magic numbers eventually would. Measured, not guessed: the
// longest label ("MITRE ATT&CK Coverage Map") is ~178px wide in the real
// Redaction font at itemLabel's 0.78rem (measured via opentype.js against
// the actual .otf — see git history), plus icon/gap/padding/rail-padding —
// sized so every label fits on one line with no ellipsis and no wrap,
// without ballooning the rail's width. There is only ONE non-hidden width
// now (no icons-only state to size separately).
const RAIL_W = '240px';

// `position: relative` purely so `handle` (now `position: absolute`, see
// below) has something to dock against — `rail` itself stays in normal flow.
const slideRow = css({
  position: 'relative',
  transform: 'translateY(-50%)',
});

// The hide/show slide is a transform on `rail` itself (translateX(-100%),
// percentage-based — not a hardcoded px value) so it fully hides regardless
// of the rail's width. Note `transform` doesn't shrink a box's LAYOUT size,
// only where it paints — `rail` still occupies its full width in the
// flex/box model while hidden, which is fine (nothing else needs its layout
// space back) and is exactly why `handle` is docked via explicit sibling
// selectors keyed off `data-view` rather than plain flex-sibling reflow (see
// `handle`'s own comment).
// `borderTop` is a 3px `primary` accent rather than the default 1px border
// color — a deliberate identity marker (see this file's own top comment on
// `railHeader`) so the panel reads as a designed widget at a glance instead
// of a flat, unstyled rectangle.
const rail = css({
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  alignItems: 'stretch',
  gap: '0.08rem',
  w: RAIL_W,
  p: '0.35rem',
  bg: 'bgCard',
  border: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
  borderLeft: 'none',
  borderTop: '3px solid token(colors.primary)',
  borderTopRightRadius: 'lg',
  borderBottomRightRadius: 'lg',
  boxShadow: 'cardHover',
  overflow: 'hidden',
  // `visibility` is part of the transition (not just `transform`) because a
  // transform alone hides NOTHING from the tab order — every link in the
  // collapsed rail stayed focusable and focus landed off-screen (WCAG 2.4.3 /
  // 2.4.7 / 2.4.11). `visibility: hidden` removes them from the tab order per
  // spec, and the delay is split across the two states exactly the way
  // `.nav-more-menu` does it in global.css: the transition on the DESTINATION
  // state governs, so the hidden rule carries the 400ms delay (stay visible
  // for the whole slide-out, then disappear) while the shown rule flips it
  // back instantly (0s) so the slide-in is actually visible.
  transition: 'transform 400ms ease, visibility 0s linear 0s',
  '&[data-view="hidden"]': {
    transform: 'translateX(-100%)',
    visibility: 'hidden',
    transition: 'transform 400ms ease, visibility 0s linear 400ms',
  },
});

// Grab handle — the rail's own first child (in normal flow, not absolutely
// positioned like `handle` below), so it automatically spans the rail's
// current width. Dragging it repositions the whole rail vertically (see the
// script's drag/keyboard/reclamp logic) — `visibility: hidden` (not
// `display: none`) while `data-view="hidden"` both hides it and removes it
// from the tab order (per spec, a `visibility: hidden` element is never
// focusable), matching the rest of the rail translating off-screen in that
// state. `touchAction: none` stops the browser's own touch-scroll gesture
// from competing with a touch drag on this element.
const dragHandle = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  justifyContent: 'center',
  w: '100%',
  h: '14px',
  mb: '0.3rem',
  borderRadius: 'sm',
  color: 'textMuted',
  cursor: 'grab',
  touchAction: 'none',
  transition: 'color 150ms ease, background 150ms ease',
  _hover: { color: 'primary', bg: 'bgSubtle' },
  '&:active': { cursor: 'grabbing' },
  '[data-view="hidden"] &': { visibility: 'hidden' },
});

// The self-explaining header — see this file's own top comment. `bg:
// 'bgSubtle'` (a different tone than `rail`'s own `bgCard`) is what gives the
// panel internal visual layering instead of one flat slab of color.
// Header row. Now that the mode-aware description is gone, this is a single
// line — so it's styled as a compact eyebrow rather than the tinted card it was
// (a 26px badge beside one short line left a lopsided block of empty space).
// A small inline icon, the title, and a hairline rule that runs to the rail's
// edge: it reads as a section header for the switch below it instead of a
// floating box competing with it.
const railHeader = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  gap: '0.45rem',
  px: '0.15rem',
  pb: '0.5rem',
  mb: '0.5rem',
  borderBottom: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
});

const railHeaderIcon = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  justifyContent: 'center',
  color: 'primary',
  flexShrink: 0,
});

const railHeaderTitle = css({
  fontFamily: 'sans',
  fontSize: '0.68rem',
  fontWeight: 700,
  textTransform: 'uppercase',
  letterSpacing: '0.1em',
  color: 'textMuted',
  lineHeight: 1.2,
  m: 0,
});

// Top-toolbar dataset switch — a real segmented switch (role="radiogroup" data-no-radiokeys of
// 3 role="radio" buttons, Reference / Tools / Drills, all three always rendered at
// once — see this file's own top comment) rather than a single icon-swap
// button. Wired into the shared, site-wide sliding-thumb system
// (`src/scripts/animatedSwitch.ts`, mounted once via AnimatedSwitch.astro in
// BaseLayout — see CLAUDE.md invariant 12's "Retrofitting a new switch needs
// exactly one change" note): the track just needs `position: 'relative',
// zIndex: 0` and each button needs `role="radio"` + a `data-active`
// attribute on whichever one is current — no per-component thumb code here
// at all, that script auto-wires every `[role="radiogroup" data-no-radiokeys]` on the page.
//
// Deliberately kept VERTICAL (a stacked column) rather than a horizontal
// row: `animatedSwitch.ts` reads a group's `aria-orientation` ONCE, at
// setup, to decide whether the thumb slides via translateX/width
// (horizontal) or translateY/height (vertical), and never re-reads it
// afterward. There's no longer a narrower "icons" width this rail ever
// shrinks to (the rail has exactly one non-hidden width now), so staying
// vertical is now simply this control's own fixed layout choice, not a
// workaround for a width that used to change out from under it.
const modeSwitchTrack = css({
  position: 'relative', // containing block + stacking context for the animated switch thumb
  zIndex: 0,
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  gap: '0.15rem',
  mb: '0.15rem',
});

// Each segment — icon + label, both always visible now (there's no icons-
// only rail width left to gate the label behind). `w: '100%'` matches
// `item`'s own explicit-percentage convention below. The `&[data-active]`
// flat-color treatment is the same JS-free fallback pattern `ModeToggle`'s
// `segBtn` uses — plain server-rendered CSS, fully correct and accessible
// with zero JS — and gets its own background/border/shadow suppressed
// automatically by the shared `[data-switch-armed] [role='radio'][data-active]`
// rule in motion.css once a thumb is actually armed (see animatedSwitch.ts's
// own header comment); the active-state `color` here is untouched by that
// rule and keeps working as the JS-free indicator no-JS/reduced-motion
// visitors rely on.
const modeSegBtn = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  gap: '0.45rem',
  w: '100%',
  h: '28px',
  px: '0.4rem',
  borderRadius: 'md',
  border: '1px solid transparent',
  bg: 'transparent',
  color: 'textMuted',
  cursor: 'pointer',
  transition: 'color 150ms ease, background 150ms ease, border-color 150ms ease',
  _hover: { color: 'primary', bg: 'bgSubtle' },
  '& svg': { flexShrink: 0 },
  '&[data-active]': {
    bg: 'bgCard',
    color: 'primary',
    borderColor: 'border',
    boxShadow: 'card',
  },
});

const modeSegLabel = css({
  fontSize: '0.72rem',
  fontWeight: 600,
  whiteSpace: 'nowrap',
  minW: 0,
});

const divider = css({ borderTop: '1px solid token(colors.border)', my: '0.3rem' });

// Fixed-height toolbar strip (drag handle, mode switch, divider) that never
// scrolls — only the category/link list below it does. `flexShrink: 0`
// keeps it from ever being compressed by the flex column it sits in
// alongside `scrollRegion`. Measured directly by the script
// (`data-tool-sidebar-toolbar`) to compute how much vertical room is left
// for the scrollable region below, rather than hardcoding a guess that would
// silently drift the moment a control here is added, removed, or resized.
const toolbar = css({
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  flexShrink: 0,
});

// The scrollable region holding every category block — see this file's own
// top comment for the full "why" behind replacing the old JS-measured
// show-all-or-disable mechanism with this. `maxHeight` reads a CSS custom
// property (`--sidebar-max-h`) the script keeps in sync with the real space
// available below the sticky header on THIS viewport (`updateScrollCap()`
// below), falling back to a generous static value for the brief pre-JS
// window and for genuine no-JS visitors — the fallback is deliberately
// generous rather than tight, since a no-JS visitor gets no refinement pass
// afterward. `overscrollBehavior: 'contain'` stops a scroll that reaches the
// top/bottom of this list from chaining into a scroll of the page body
// behind it. `pr` gives the native scrollbar (when one appears) a sliver of
// breathing room instead of sitting flush against link text.
const scrollRegion = css({
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  gap: '0.08rem',
  overflowY: 'auto',
  overflowX: 'hidden',
  overscrollBehavior: 'contain',
  maxHeight: 'var(--sidebar-max-h, calc(100dvh - 220px))',
  pr: '0.15rem',
});

// Wraps one category's header + link list together so a single `hidden`
// toggle controls both atomically (see the script's `applyGroupVisibility`)
// — the alternative, syncing two separately-hidden siblings, is one more
// place for the header and its links to drift out of sync.
const catBlock = css({
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  gap: '0.1rem',
  // Anchors `catAccentBar` below (position:absolute) to this block, not the
  // whole rail — so each category's colored rail spans just its own header
  // + links, not the full stack. `pl` makes room for it sitting flush at
  // this block's own left edge.
  position: 'relative',
  pl: '0.5rem',
  '&[hidden]': { display: 'none' },
});

// Colored left-rail marking each category's own section — a separate,
// isolated, childless element rather than a border directly on `catBlock`
// itself: `filter: hue-rotate()` (applied inline per-category, see
// HUES_BY_MODE) recolors an element's ENTIRE rendered subtree, so putting it
// on `catBlock` would also shift every link icon/label inside — putting it
// on this empty decorative bar instead means only the bar itself gets
// recolored.
const catAccentBar = css({
  display: 'block',
  position: 'absolute',
  left: 0,
  top: '0.1rem',
  bottom: '0.05rem',
  w: '2px',
  borderRadius: 'full',
  bg: 'primary',
});

const navGroup = css({
  display: 'flex',
  flexDirection: 'column',
  gap: '0.05rem',
});

// Always visible now — every category is always on screen, so a category
// label is never optional the way it used to be behind an "expanded" toggle.
const catHeader = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  gap: '0.4rem',
  fontSize: '0.68rem',
  fontWeight: 700,
  textTransform: 'uppercase',
  letterSpacing: '0.05em',
  color: 'textMuted',
  whiteSpace: 'nowrap',
  m: 0,
  mt: '0.3rem',
});

// Small solid color-coded dot — the per-category identity marker next to
// each `catHeader`'s own label. `boxShadow` here (not a separate glow
// element) picks up the same `filter: hue-rotate()` applied inline
// per-instance, since filter recolors an element's whole rendered output
// including its own shadow — a soft glow in the dot's own color for free, no
// extra markup.
const catDot = css({
  display: 'inline-block',
  w: '7px',
  h: '7px',
  borderRadius: 'full',
  bg: 'primary',
  boxShadow: '0 0 4px 0 token(colors.primary)',
  flexShrink: 0,
});

const item = css({
  display: 'flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  gap: '0.45rem',
  w: '100%',
  h: '28px',
  px: '0.4rem',
  borderRadius: 'md',
  color: 'textMuted',
  transition: 'color 150ms ease, background 150ms ease',
  _hover: { color: 'text', bg: 'bgSubtle' },
  '&[aria-current="page"]': { color: 'primary', bg: 'bgSubtle' },
  '& svg': { flexShrink: 0 },
});

// Wraps each link's own <Icon>, carrying the same per-category
// `hue-rotate()` inline style as `catDot`/`catAccentBar` (see HUES_BY_MODE).
// Deliberately NOT applied to `item` itself — same reasoning as
// `catAccentBar`'s own comment: `filter` recolors an element's whole
// rendered subtree, and `item` also contains the label text, which must
// stay in `item`'s own resting/hover/active color, not get hue-shifted. The
// effect is quiet by design rather than a second, competing color system:
// `currentColor` at rest is `item`'s muted `textMuted`, which is
// low-saturation enough that rotating its hue is barely perceptible — the
// tint only really reads once `item` reaches its `[aria-current="page"]`
// state, where `color: primary` is a fully saturated theme color and the
// same rotation becomes clearly visible.
const itemIcon = css({ display: 'inline-flex', flexShrink: 0 });

// Always visible now — no icons-only rail width left to gate this behind.
// Kept in the DOM always (rather than JS-inserted) so a screen reader
// always has real link text, not just aria-label. No truncation: `RAIL_W`
// is sized (see its own comment) so every label — including the longest,
// "MITRE ATT&CK Coverage Map" — fits on this single nowrap line with no
// ellipsis. Never add overflow/textOverflow here; widen RAIL_W instead if a
// longer label is ever added.
const itemLabel = css({
  fontSize: '0.78rem',
  whiteSpace: 'nowrap',
  minW: 0,
});

// `position: absolute` (docked against `slideRow`, not flex-adjacent to
// `rail`) is deliberate: `left` and `height` are driven by two EXPLICIT,
// mutually-exclusive rules keyed off `rail`'s own `data-view` attribute (the
// `[data-tool-sidebar-rail][data-view="…"] ~ &` sibling selectors below) —
// one per real state — rather than inferring position from rail's live
// flex-box width the way a plain flex sibling would. That's the fix for the
// handle landing in the wrong spot: `rail`'s hide is a `transform`, which
// repositions where it PAINTS but doesn't shrink its LAYOUT width (see
// `rail`'s comment), so a flex-sibling handle used to dock wherever rail's
// width happened to be *before* it was last hidden, instead of always
// sitting flush at the true visible edge.
//
// Small (32px) by default/hidden — just tall enough to be a comfortable tap
// target — and grows to the rail's full height whenever the rail is
// actually showing, animated via the same sibling-selector mechanism.
// `height: 100%` resolves against `slideRow`'s height, which (`handle` is
// out of normal flow) is dictated purely by `rail`'s own intrinsic content
// height, so this tracks correctly as the link list's own scroll-capped
// height changes.
// The hide/show tab. Deliberately the SAME compact size in both states and
// vertically centred against the rail, rather than the full-height strip this
// used to be: a 22px-wide bar stretched to the rail's entire height put a tiny
// chevron floating in the middle of a long blank slab, which read as a stray
// edge artifact rather than a control. A small pill with a clear chevron is
// easier to spot AND easier to understand in both states. Because the height
// is now fixed, the `--rail-h` custom property and the ResizeObserver that
// used to keep it in sync with rail's live height are gone entirely.
const handle = css({
  position: 'absolute',
  top: '50%',
  left: RAIL_W,
  transform: 'translateY(-50%)',
  display: 'inline-flex',
  alignItems: 'center',
  justifyContent: 'center',
  w: '24px',
  h: '64px',
  bg: 'bgCard',
  color: 'textMuted',
  border: '1px solid token(colors.border)',
  borderLeft: 'none',
  borderTopRightRadius: 'lg',
  borderBottomRightRadius: 'lg',
  boxShadow: 'card',
  cursor: 'pointer',
  transition: 'color 150ms ease, background 150ms ease, left 300ms ease',
  _hover: { color: 'primary', bg: 'bgSubtle' },
  _focusVisible: { color: 'primary', outline: '2px solid token(colors.primary)', outlineOffset: '2px' },
  '[data-tool-sidebar-rail][data-view="hidden"] ~ &': { left: 0 },
  // Explicit rotate(0deg) base (not just the absence of a transform rule) so
  // both endpoints of the toggle are concrete values for the transition to
